issue_context.body:
# Problem

According to the [documentation](https://docs.openssl.org/master/man3/EVP_EncodeInit), for every four valid base64 bytes processed (ignoring whitespace, carriage returns and line feeds), EVP_DecodeUpdate() produces three bytes of binary output data (except at the end of data terminated with one or two padding characters).
However, the function behaved like an EVP_DecodeBlock(): produces exactly three output bytes for every four input bytes. Such behaviour can cause writes to a non-allocated output buffer if a user allocates its size based on the documentation and knowing the padding size.

Note: BoringSSL [does not produce padding zeros](https://github.com/google/boringssl/blob/0147d7f28324c5b2923cd1e412e8aaf74124622c/crypto/base64/base64.cc#L245-L296).

# Example

After reading the documentation, I expect the following code to work. Nonetheless, it fails in the specified points.

```c
#include <openssl/evp.h>

int main()
{
    const unsigned char encoded[] = "aA==";
    unsigned char decoded[] = {0xff, 0xff, 0xff, 0xff};

    char residual_buffer[3];
    int bytes_produced = 0;
    EVP_ENCODE_CTX *ctx = EVP_ENCODE_CTX_new();
    EVP_DecodeInit(ctx);
    EVP_DecodeUpdate(ctx, decoded, &bytes_produced, encoded, sizeof(encoded) - 1);
    OPENSSL_assert(bytes_produced == 1);
    OPENSSL_assert(decoded[0] == 'h');
    // Fails because padding zeros are produced.
    OPENSSL_assert(decoded[1] == 0xff);
    // Fails because padding zeros are produced.
    OPENSSL_assert(decoded[2] == 0xff);
    OPENSSL_assert(decoded[3] == 0xff);
    EVP_DecodeFinal(ctx, residual_buffer, &bytes_produced);
    EVP_ENCODE_CTX_free(ctx);
    OPENSSL_assert(bytes_produced == 0);

    return 0;
}
```



# Workaround

A workaround to this problem is to create an intermediate buffer that can handle the needed size, decode the message there, and then copy its contents to the structure.

# Why it is important

Use case: a user needs to validate a message.
They create a structure containing SHA256 digest, 64-byte digital signature, and text.
The digest and signature sizes are known beforehand, so they can be stored in static arrays.

The problem is that the SHA256 digest is 32 bytes long, which is not a multiple of three.
A 64-byte signature size is also not divisible by three.
According to the documentation, it should not be a problem as long as `EVP_DecodeInit()`, `EVP_DecodeUpdate()`, and `EVP_DecodeFinal()` are used instead of `EVP_DecodeBlock()`.
However, this is not the case, and this may give a lot of headaches to the user.

```c
#include <openssl/evp.h>
#include <openssl/sha.h>

struct Message
{
    unsigned char digest[SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH];
    unsigned char signature[512 / 8];
    const char* text;
};

int main()
{
    const unsigned char signature[] = "9ZCgtJAZATw1F/O9z3MErfMW3+pEzb7DJDZ1KJEVs3NKAO0wC2/uZ8gzHbiSvfgtuAaNyLi+NH4qD476/RZsKQ==";
    const unsigned char digest[] = "7hlqbgoDiuIm+1XvznYv3w6lpMFlQROmAcAWG4pULFk=";
    char text[] = "Hello world!";
    struct Message message;
    message.text = text;

    char residual_buffer[3];
    int bytes_produced = 0;
    EVP_ENCODE_CTX *ctx = NULL;

    ctx = EVP_ENCODE_CTX_new();
    EVP_DecodeInit(ctx);
    EVP_DecodeUpdate(ctx, message.signature, &bytes_produced, signature, sizeof(signature) - 1);
    OPENSSL_assert(bytes_produced == sizeof(message.signature));
    OPENSSL_assert(message.signature[0] != 0);
    // Fails because padding zeros are written to the `message.text` pointer.
    OPENSSL_assert(message.text == text);
    EVP_DecodeFinal(ctx, residual_buffer, &bytes_produced);
    EVP_ENCODE_CTX_free(ctx);
    OPENSSL_assert(bytes_produced == 0);

    ctx = EVP_ENCODE_CTX_new();
    EVP_DecodeInit(ctx);
    EVP_DecodeUpdate(ctx, message.digest, &bytes_produced, digest, sizeof(digest) - 1);
    OPENSSL_assert(bytes_produced == sizeof(message.digest));
    // Fails because padding zeros are written to the `message.signature` array
    OPENSSL_assert(message.signature[0] != 0);
    // Fails because padding zeros are written to the `message.text` pointer.
    OPENSSL_assert(message.text == text);
    EVP_DecodeFinal(ctx, residual_buffer, &bytes_produced);
    EVP_ENCODE_CTX_free(ctx);
    OPENSSL_assert(bytes_produced == 0);

    return 0;
}
```

Checked on Ubuntu 20 with [libssl-dev](https://launchpad.net/ubuntu/focal/+package/libssl-dev) 1.1.1. Build command: `gcc main.c -lssl -lcrypto`.

# History

There was a pull request https://github.com/openssl/openssl/pull/17217 attempting to fix such behaviour in `EVP_DecodeBlock()`. However, it was closed because `EVP_DecodeBlock()` is de-facto obsolete.

There were also issues https://github.com/openssl/openssl/issues/17197 and https://github.com/openssl/openssl/issues/23863 saying that `EVP_DecodeBlock()` produces strange padding.

In these cases,  `EVP_DecodeInit()`, `EVP_DecodeUpdate()`, and `EVP_DecodeFinal()` were suggested as high-level modern safe alternatives. Nevertheless, they do not follow the documentation (at least my understanding of it).
